@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-expectations Expectations
@brief Expectations are checks whose outcomes are counted and reported.

@details
`t.expect (condition)` checks a condition, counts the result, and reports
it; when the check fails, the test case continues, so that all its checks
are evaluated and reported in one run.

The condition is one of:
- a function comparator, such as `mt::eq (actual, 42)`, or a combination
  of them, which reports the values of its operands;
- an exception check, such as `mt::throws (...)`;
- any value that converts to `bool`, such as `x == 1` or a
  `std::optional`, which reports only the location of the failed check.

A message can be appended with `<<`, with any values that can be written,
for example `t.expect (x == 2) << "x is " << x;`. The check is reported at
the end of the statement, with the message, or, without one, with the
condition.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check the values", [] (auto& t) {
  int x = 1;
  t.expect (mt::eq (x, 2));
  t.expect (x == 2) << "x is " << x;
});
@endcode

Both checks fail; the human-readable reporter displays:

@code{.console}
    • Check the values
        ✗ FAILED (main.cpp:13, 1 == 2)
        ✗ x is 1 FAILED (main.cpp:14)
@endcode
